  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Inclusive and Transformative Extractives Industries in Southern Africa</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Inclusive and Accountable Extractive Industries project (2017 -2020) aims to improve regional natural resource governance in Malawi, Mozambique, South Africa, Zambia and Zimbabwe. By June 2020 the project aims to achieve government, policy and legislative changes relating to the generation, collection and expenditure of EI revenues, for example passing disclosure laws, or revising double taxation and investment agreements. The project also seeks to promote greater human rights adherence from governments and companies, for example through adopting Free Prior Informed Consent (FPIC), gender, resettlement and compensation policies; and support civil society organisations (CSOs) to operate without victimisation and harassment, and to participate in and benefit from all areas of the sector. Finally, the project aims to support communities and governments develop roadmaps to decrease financial dependence on EI. The project will particularly focus on the impact of EI on women, with a particular emphasis on women impacted by artisanal small scale mining (ASM) given the high number of women in the sector and the threats (violence) and opportunities (income) they face. In Zimbabwe By working in 4 districts ( Gwanda, Marange, Shurugwi and Zvishavane), this action will address limited capacities, structures, processes that hinder CSOs to organize, speak out and act against poverty, inequality, injustice in the mining sector. Oxfam proposes a project that addresses the participation and engagement of communities in the development of Zimbabwe&apos;s mining sector, while leveraging national influence on the government and the industry as a whole.   Business and Human rights: Oxfam will support initiatives for adoption and implementation of Business and Human Rights (BHR); awareness raising, community based monitoring and multi -stakeholder engagement and consensus building. Revenue transparency: Through evidence based research, analysis and capacity building we will strengthen the c</narrative>
  </description>
